WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.220
Ari Aster's much-anticipated follow-up to
Hereditary, is finally here, so horror fans

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.060
everywhere can dig into this sun-drenched
piece of folk-horror.

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:12.600
Let's look at why Midsommar just might end
up being the scariest movie of 2019.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:13.360
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.040
Midsommar has been heavily marketed as a horror
film that will scare you despite taking place

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.060
almost entirely in broad daylight.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.920
Midsommar isn't the first film to do this,
of course, as any fan of films like The Wicker

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.700
Man or Day of the Dead can attest.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.840
It's not some kind of revolutionary breakthrough
for horror, but as with many things in this

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.400
film, it's executed flawlessly.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.840
As the film progresses, the daylight becomes
such a threatening specter that you find yourself

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.660
almost praying for a little more darkness.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.932
At least the darkness might cover up some
of the gruesome events you're forced to see

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.010
in bright detail.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:42.920
What time is it?

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.120
Nine PM."

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.120
"That can't be right, the sky is blue!"

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.660
"This is what nine PM is like here."

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.579
Midsommar is a film that largely had to be
built from the ground up.

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:55.489
The village had to be assembled piece by piece,
from the murals along the walls of the common

00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:59.200
sleeping area to that ominous yellow triangle
temple where it all comes to an end.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.950
The village is an outgrowth of the film's
mission to be a horrific movie experience

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.140
that takes place almost entirely in daylight.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.500
It has to look like a place of celebration,
because for the people who live there, it

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.000
is.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.300
It also has to subtly look deeply ominous.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:14.540
It has to look like a festival destination
even as it becomes a prison.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.320
The daylight of Midsommar carries with it
an ominous mission statement.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.000
You can close or cover your eyes.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:24.300
None of its moments of emotional and physical
darkness are matched with aesthetic darkness.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.220
This is a bright film that will not look away.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:29.540
Aster's camerawork in the film reinforces
this mission statement.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.280
While he certainly doesn't show us everything,
the visual style of the film suggests a filmmaker

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.860
who wants to be an all-knowning eye in this
world he's created.

00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:40.999
That extends into the film's most graphically
violent sequences, and moments in which another

00:01:40.999 --> 00:01:45.200
filmmaker might cut away are instead pushed
in on, more and more, until we're the ones

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:45.760
who flinch.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.740
In Hereditary, Aster gave us everything from
a little girl's head flying off to a decapitated

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.460
corpse floating up into a tree house.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.650
The man is not afraid to pile on the gore,
and that continues in Midsommar, a film that

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.080
brings that gore into the harsh daylight as
often as possible.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.340
Simply saying Aster is a director who enjoys
putting gruesome moments in his films isn't

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:04.040
saying much.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.099
A lot of horror directors have accumulated
fame thanks to their relentless use of bucket

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.260
after bucket of blood.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:14.060
What makes Aster's particularly gore style
effective is how judicious he is with it.

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:18.040
Aster knows exactly how long to wait before
dropping his deeply upsetting gore on us,

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.540
and his timing is what makes the violence
work so well.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:25.200
As relentless as Midsommar is in its assault
on your senses, Aster knows from the beginning

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.260
that there needs to be some way to release
the tension.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:31.620
Much like another horror classic, Get Out,
Midsommar uses humor for cool-down moments

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.780
and flashes of levity.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.580
It all makes the ramp up to the real horror
even scarier.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.600
This is where the often unexpected and very
welcome sense of humor of Midsommar comes

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:40.100
in.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.000
This humor often lives largely in the character
of Mark, played by Will Poulter.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:47.519
He's more than happy to chase women around
the village while puffing on his vape pen

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.460
and unknowingly taking a leak on the ashes
of their ancestors.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.659
Thanks to him, the film adds the horror of
American tourists as an invasive species into

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:55.340
its narrative.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.220
Midsommar is a creeper, in more ways than
one.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.320
It's a film that's not interested in delivering
a scare-a-minute thrill ride.

00:03:02.329 --> 00:03:05.909
Instead it creates a sense of dread that runs
through the whole piece like a taut wire,

00:03:05.909 --> 00:03:08.780
occasionally building to moments where you
feel the wire might snap.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.120
"Tomorrow's a big day."

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:11.520
"Is it scary?"

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.469
The May Queen dance competition near the end
of the film, in which Dani is drugged and

00:03:15.469 --> 00:03:19.780
coerced into a dance endurance ritual, is
a master class in this kind of filmmaking.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:23.840
As the dance goes on, she is swept along in
the ecstasy of the movement, and the constantly

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.780
twirling tangle of women around the Maypole
becomes a kind of transformative machine for

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.320
Dani, helping her to begin to become something
else.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:34.420
Through it all, there's a sense that at any
moment the scene could explode into hideous,

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:35.629
relentlessly graphic violence.

00:03:36.629 --> 00:03:40.170
Midsommar does not break the mold established
by other folk-horror classics that have come

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:40.860
before it.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.720
We know the writing is on the wall for our
American visitors well before they do, we

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.160
just don't yet know how they'll come to meet
their various ends.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:51.540
Even when it becomes clear that Pelle has
selected Dani as a candidate for May Queen,

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.410
we don't know if that means she's marked to
die or marked to somehow survive and assimilate.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:59.060
Then, as the film reaches its end, everything
is laid out rather clearly.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:03.730
Dani survives and she uses her newfound adoration
among the people of the village to make her

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.880
boyfriend Christian their final sacrifice.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:10.480
The preparation of the ritual and the unflinching
camera work are all designed to make you nervous.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.540
Then the screaming starts, and those nerves
are released in an ecstasy of primal energy

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.320
that feels at once celebratory and horrifying.

